# App Traverse coding-agent rules

## Incremental build only

Never, unless the user explicitly asks:

- rebuild, clean, `--clean-first`
- deleting the build directory
- clearing the CMake cache or dependency cache
- re-downloading / rebuilding all dependencies
- `git clean` for the sake of a build

Use only incremental builds of the existing build tree. If the tree is broken, explain the concrete cause. Do not destroy it automatically.

## No C++ RTTI

AppTraverse code must compile with C++ RTTI disabled (`/GR-` on MSVC,
`-fno-rtti` on GCC/Clang). Applied centrally via `apptraverse_compile_policy`.

Forbidden:

- `dynamic_cast`
- `typeid`
- `std::type_info`
- `std::type_index`
- any design relying on compiler RTTI

Use instead:

- Æther object class IDs;
- `ae::Registry`;
- `Registry::GenerationDistance`;
- typed `ae::Ptr<T>` / `ae::ObjPtr<T>`;
- `Ptr::as<T>()` only where architecture already guarantees the concrete type;
- virtual dispatch where behavior belongs to an interface/base class.

Do not re-enable RTTI for a target to work around code design.
If code cannot compile without RTTI, fix that code.

`dynamic_cast` is forbidden in AppTraverse production and test code.
To test whether an `ae::Obj` is a derived object-system class: use
`Registry::GenerationDistance(Base::kClassId, obj.GetClassId())`, then
`static_cast` only after the relation is proven. When the concrete type is
already guaranteed by a reflected field / object graph, use typed
`ObjPtr` / `Ptr` conversion.

## Fail fast on global invariants

If a global invariant is broken and the app cannot work, fail immediately at
the origin. `assert` is stripped under `NDEBUG`; fatal checks at a real failure
boundary (`RegisterClassW`, `CreateWindowExW`, `CreateEventW`, `PostMessageW`,
`SetEvent`, `DestroyWindow`, `CloseHandle`, `WAIT_FAILED`, required
`LoadApplication`) must use a live check and `abort`. Capture `GetLastError`
immediately on Win32 and pass it to the reused `FatalWin32` helper in the
Windows example. `WriteFatalStderr` writes once: CRT stderr when attached,
otherwise `STD_ERROR_HANDLE`. Do not compile registrar TUs with `NDEBUG` in a
Debug link of aether-objects: `Factory` layout is `#ifndef NDEBUG`. Test names
must match the actual compile configuration; a Debug missing-load test is not
an NDEBUG/Release proof. No modal dialog, retries, or recovery. Do not wrap
the operation in `assert`. Do not duplicate `if (!x)` then `assert(x)`.

Examples: required model/Domain/root failed to load; main native window was not
created; required graph reference or class registration is missing.

- Real state-machine alternative → ordinary `if`
- Globally impossible / fatal state → fail at the origin, then treat the invariant as true
- Do not re-check it in every later function
- Do not write `if (!x) return` / `if (hwnd)` when the previous stage already guaranteed `x`

Before each defensive check: can this state actually be reached after earlier stages succeeded? If not, remove the check.

## Do not re-check established invariants

Once a lifecycle / load / graph boundary establishes an invariant, downstream
code treats it as true.

Forbidden patterns unless the state is a genuine runtime alternative:

- `if (!ptr) return;`
- `if (!obj.is_valid()) return;`
- `if (!obj.is_loaded()) return;`
- `if (hwnd != nullptr) ...`
- `if (presenter != nullptr) ...`

when an earlier successful stage already guarantees the condition.

Do not turn corrupted graph / programming errors into silent no-ops.

Distinguish:

**Real state alternative:** stale Item no longer in live list; no unread
publication; stop requested; optional user state.

**Programming / global invariant:** required graph reference missing; required
presenter absent; presenter invoked before required graph loaded; native handle
absent after successful OnLoad; proxy target missing; object class mismatch.

For programming / global invariants: check/fail at the boundary where the
invariant is established; after that do not repeat defensive checks.

Do not write `if (!x) return;` then later assume `x`. Do not write both
`if (!x) fatal;` and later `assert(x);` at subsequent layers.

## No defensive programming

AppTraverse follows invariant-driven code, not defensive programming.

Do not add checks for hypothetical states unless there is a concrete reachable
state transition that produces them.

Every `if` guarding an invariant must answer: "What valid execution path
reaches this condition?" If none: remove the branch.

This applies to future platform ports (Win32, macOS, Linux, Android, iOS, WASM).

## Registration: two different things

`APPTRAVERSE_REGISTER` / `EnsureObjectRegistration` registers Æther object
classes. `WNDCLASSW` / `RegisterClassW` registers a native Windows window class.
They are not interchangeable. The native Main class, WndProc, HWND create and
destroy belong to `Win32MainWindowPresenter`. Loading/notify classes belong to
`WinApp::Run` for that process run.

If classes/subsystems must be registered before worker threads: once, at one obvious startup point, before those threads start. Do not call `Ensure...Registration()` on several layers “just in case”. If a call is already guaranteed earlier, do not repeat it below.

## Synchronization

Simplifying helpers must not drop mutex coverage around a condition-variable
predicate. Mutations that waiters observe (`stop`, `has_unread_published`) are
done under the same mutex as `wait`, then `notify`. `ModelSession` has no
platform handles. Publication is reported through the required `on_published`
callback after the mutex is released. Windows signals completion (`SetEvent`)
only after `Run` returns, in the thread lambda, once graph/Domain/storage have
left scope.

## Do not invent entities

Do not add Manager, Service, Coordinator, Context, Runtime wrapper, a registry on top of the existing registry, Lifecycle abstraction, Controller, Repository, helper object, or extra state machine without a real need.

Use App Traverse primitives, aether-objects, platform APIs, locals, RAII, and straight-line control flow. A new type is allowed only if it is a real domain concept, is actually reused, or ownership/lifetime is worse without it. No abstractions “for later”. A small free function is allowed when several call sites share the same operation (for example `FatalWin32`).

## Inline small single-use functions

If a function is small, called from one place, is not a callback/API boundary, and does not hide a genuinely complex operation: inline it and put a short intent comment on the block.

Leave callbacks, WndProc, real algorithms, and reused functions separate. This is not code golf.

## Tests must not shape production

Do not add fields, stage enums, `hold_stage`, raw object addresses, extra atomics, `WasLoaded`/`WasDistilled` getters, test-only hooks, or artificial barriers to production classes for the convenience of tests. Tests check observable production behavior. Keep test infrastructure in test code.

## Distillation is development-only

Distillation / initial-state creation is not production. A load-only/production build only loads persisted state; it does not create state or fall back to distillation. A dev build may, if state is missing: build the initial graph, distill, save, then load via the normal production load path. Dev-only code must be behind a compile-time macro/target and must not appear in the load-only lifecycle path.

## Main-window example startup / shutdown

Startup (not cancelable; user cannot close the app before Main): GUI thread creates Loading → start model thread → model fully loads → initial mirror published → GUI mirror → presenters init → Main created → Loading destroyed.

Shutdown only after full startup: close Main → wake model thread → model frees
graph/Domain/storage on that thread → `Run` returns → Windows `SetEvent` → GUI
join → GUI frees presentation and GUI Domain → exit.

Do not restore close-during-Loading without an explicit assignment.

## Always commit and push

After every finished iteration: commit and push the current working branch. Do not leave finished work local-only. If some tests are blocked, MCP is down, or an external service is unavailable: still commit, push, and say what was not verified.

Do not change `main`, merge, or rewrite published history without an explicit request.

## Supply constants vs dynamic objects (Events only)

The model changes **only through Events**. Supply-constant objects must not be
mutated by Events or Apply code you add or change.

`OverlayDomainStorage` (`include/apptraverse/overlay_domain_storage.h`)
decides **where layers load and save** (const bodies stay on BACKING, not USER).
It does **not** validate Event correctness at runtime. Do **not** add runtime
const checks in `Node`, `Commit`, `Apply`, `Domain`, or `ae::Env`.

When you create or modify an Event, `Apply`, or any other model mutation path:

1. Identify every object and field whose persisted state would change.
2. From the supplied graph description (fixture, PLAN/PROGRESS, distill/manifest
   docs when present), classify each affected `ObjId` as supply-constant or
   dynamic. One C++ type can be both roles on different instances.
3. Follow nested references and aliases (`ObjPtr`, `ObjectLink`, shared
   `ObjId`), not only the Event’s primary target type.
4. **Reject** changes that mutate a supply-constant object. Fix the source;
   do not add runtime guards instead.
5. If you cannot classify an object statically, say so explicitly and do not
   treat the review as complete.

Check direct assignments and methods that change materialized state. Cursor
review and human code review are the enforcement boundary; they are not a formal
immutability proof.

## Prototype: no transport-file hardening

AppTraverse is a **prototype**. Unless the user explicitly asks for it, **do not add**:

- file magic / signatures;
- format version fields used only to reject files;
- CRC / checksum / hash integrity for on-disk artifacts;
- truncated / trailing / corrupt-input detection;
- malformed-input classification or fail-closed parsers for our own tools' output;
- size / count limits for defensive rejection;
- sorted / unique / canonical validation on read;
- duplicate-key validation on read;
- atomic apply / rollback / retry / fallback for damaged packages;
- fuzz or negative tests for corrupted files;
- golden SHA of binaries to prove file correctness;
- analysis of whether a file was written correctly or damaged.

Assume files are produced by **our** exporters and are correct. Implement **happy path** plus **application-semantics** checks (labels, USER vs const, ObjId, compatibility). Wrong arbitrary files are out of scope until a dedicated task.

## Æther-only wire formats

For AppTraverse, only **standard** Æther/AppTraverse serialization is allowed (`BinaryArchive`, object graph, existing storage). Do **not** introduce parallel wire formats, custom containers, hand-rolled serializers, or extra metadata layers on top of the object system unless the user explicitly requires it.

## No hidden refactors

Keep each iteration small and reviewable. Do not “fix” neighboring architecture in passing. If you find a separate problem: record it in `plan.md` / `Progress.md` as TODO and leave it for a later iteration.

## Typed object references (not bare integer identity)

In the model and runtime graph, object identity is represented by `T::ptr`,
`ae::ObjPtr<T>`, `ae::Ptr<T>`, or `T&` when lifetime is already guaranteed.

Do not use `std::uint32_t` as object identity. `ae::ObjId` is allowed only at
real boundaries: storage/serialization keys, wire/publication payloads, Model
Domain ↔ UI Domain handoff, intentional unloaded lookup, fixed distill/test
IDs.

Forbidden: storing both `ObjPtr` and a copied ID for the same object; passing
IDs between functions in one domain when a typed ptr exists; replacing model
relationships with ID fields; `ObjId → uint32_t → ObjId`; ID lookup after the
object is already available as ptr/reference. Raw IDs on allowed boundaries stay
`ae::ObjId`, not integer. ClassId, schema version, storage keys, and fixed
distill ObjIds are not “file magic” and are not removed.

## Required graph is all-or-nothing

After a successful build/load boundary, the required graph is complete. No
cascade guards on required refs (`if (!catalog) return`, repeated
`is_valid()`/`is_loaded()` on the same chain, `Require…Wiring`, repair after
load). Wire required refs at composition, persist, load, then use directly.
Production does not re-validate the same chain everywhere; model/headless tests
do. Keep `if` only for real runtime alternatives (optional selection, lazy
unload, missing checkpoint, stop, no publication, pre-`GWLP_USERDATA` WndProc,
invalid user index). Native handles are checked once at acquisition.

## Trusted prototype storage (WindowCatalog-only build)

Prototype files are trusted. Do not add or keep in the active path: magic/header
signatures, checksums, integrity verification, recovery/repair/backup, retries,
atomic `.tmp` + rename, duplicated stream-state checks, storage commit
statistics, or fallback to a default graph after failed load. Allowed branches:
optional file/storage/tombstone present vs absent; if an optional file exists,
read it straight and treat as correct. Do not remove serializer size/count
fields. Do not apply this policy to versioning/distillation integration; it
applies to the current WindowCatalog-only prototype build.

## plan.md / Progress.md

Before each task, read `plan.md`, `Progress.md`, and these repository instructions.

- `plan.md`: architecture decisions, next-stage order, deferred work
- `Progress.md`: what was done, commit SHA, test/MCP results, known limitations, push status

Do not mark accepted-by-user yourself.
